{"ts": 0.058911, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
{"ts": 0.061043, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97m4.1 — reading a command\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nA shell command has a small grammar:\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mcommand  options  arguments\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n  \u001b[92mls\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mls -l\u001b[0m                    \u001b[90m# one option, no argument\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mls -l /etc\u001b[0m               \u001b[90m# option + argument\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mwc -l poem.txt\u001b[0m           \u001b[90m# count lines in a file\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mecho \"hello world\"\u001b[0m       \u001b[90m# argument with a space — quoted\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[91mSpaces matter.\u001b[0m  The shell splits on spaces. If a filename\r\nhas a space, \u001b[36mquote it\u001b[0m or the shell will see two arguments:\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mvim \"my notes.txt\"\u001b[0m       \u001b[90m# opens one file\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mvim my notes.txt\u001b[0m         \u001b[90m# opens TWO files: 'my' and 'notes.txt'\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[36mcommand -v\u001b[0m asks the shell whether a command exists.\r\n  \u001b[92mcommand -v ls\u001b[0m            \u001b[90m# prints the path; exits 0\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mcommand -v vim\u001b[0m           \u001b[90m# does Vim exist on this system?\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mcommand -v vimtutor\u001b[0m      \u001b[90m# does vimtutor?\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Try a f", "len": 1024}
{"ts": 0.061128, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "ew \u001b[92mcommand -v\u001b[93m probes, then \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 58}
